{"records":[{"id":"b2n5f3wvzg","title":"ATG5 provides host protection acting as a switch in the atg8ylation cascade between autophagy and secretion","description":"ATG5 is a part of the E3 ligase directing lipidation of ATG8 proteins, a process central to membrane atg8ylation and canonical autophagy. Loss of Atg5 in myeloid cells causes early mortality in murine models of tuberculosis. This in vivo phenotype is specific to ATG5. Here we show using human cell lines that absence of ATG5 but not of other ATGs directing canonical autophagy promotes lysosomal exocytosis and secretion of extracellular vesicles, and, in murine Atg5fl/fl LysM-Cre neutrophils, their excessive degranulation. This is due to lysosomal disrepair in ATG5 knockout cells and the sequestration by an alternative conjugation complex, ATG12-ATG3, of ESCRT protein ALIX which acts in membrane repair and exosome secretion. These findings reveal a previously undescribed function of ATG5 in its host-protective role in murine experimental models of tuberculosis and emphasize the significance of the branching aspects of the atg8ylation conjugation cascade beyond the canonical autophagy.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-17T07:51:18.474Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b2n5f3wvzg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b2n5f3wvzg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eric","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Autophagy","Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b93drbrj87","title":"Raw dataset for LysopH-up","description":"Raw dataset for \"Optogenetic Control of Lysosomal Exocytosis Reveals Cerebellar LTP as a Substrate for Gain-Down Oculomotor Learning\"\nby Wataru Kakegawa, Itaru Arai, Takao Imai, Shigetomo Suyama, Kazuya Nozawa, Junko Motohashi, Eriko Miura, Rei Abe-Yoshizumi, Satoshi P. Tsunoda, Hiroshi Nonaka, Itaru Hamachi, Hideki Kandori, Michisuke Yuzaki, Shinji Matsuda","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-16T11:46:48Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b93drbrj87","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b93drbrj87"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michisuke","last_name":"Yuzaki","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Neuroscience","Electrophysiology","Immunohistochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z6bgky278p","title":"SARS-CoV-2 N protein mediates intercellular nucleic acid dispersion, a feature reduced in Omicron. Wu et al.","description":"SARS-CoV-2 N protein-RNA complex entering into HPAEpiC cells by lattice light sheet microscopy, which demonstrates that N protein carries nucleic acid into cells and mediates intercellular nucleic acid dispersion.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-09T10:34:38.498Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z6bgky278p","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z6bgky278p"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jung-Lin","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"I-I","last_name":"Kuan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ft63v4p2cp","title":"A specific form of cPRC1 containing CBX4 is co-opted to mediate oncogenic gene repression in diffuse midline glioma","description":"Diffuse midline glioma (DMG) is a fatal childhood brain tumour characterised primarily by mutant histone H3 (H3K27M). H3K27M causes a global reduction in Polycomb Repressive Complex 2 (PRC2)-mediated H3K27me3. Paradoxically, PRC2 is essential in DMG cells, although the downstream molecular mechanisms are poorly understood. Here, we’ve discovered a specific form of canonical PRC1 (cPRC1) containing CBX4 and PCGF4 drives oncogenic gene repression downstream of H3K27me3 in DMG cells. Via a novel functional region, CBX4 preferentially associates with PCGF4 containing cPRC1. The characteristic H3K27me3 landscape in DMG rewires the distribution of cPRC1 complexes, with CBX4/PCGF4-cPRC1 accumulating at H3K27me3 enriched CpG islands. Despite comprising <5% of cPRC1 in DMG cells, the unique repressive functions of CBX4/PCGF4-cPRC1 are essential for DMG growth. Our findings link the altered distribution of H3K27me3 to imbalanced cPRC1 function, which drives oncogenic gene repression in DMG, highlighting potential therapeutic opportunities for this incurable childhood brain cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-23T09:04:34.191Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ft63v4p2cp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ft63v4p2cp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Eimear","last_name":"Lagan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Dáire","last_name":"Gannon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"AJ","last_name":"Silva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Bibawi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anthony ","last_name":"Doherty","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Darragh","last_name":"Nimmo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rachel","last_name":"McCole","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Craig ","last_name":"Monger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giovani","last_name":"Genesi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aurelie","last_name":"Vanderlinden","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Innes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charlotte","last_name":"Jones","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bryan","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guido","last_name":"van Mierlo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pascal","last_name":"Jansen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chinmayi","last_name":"Pednekar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"von Kriegsheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keiran ","last_name":"Wynne","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisco","last_name":"Sánchez-Rivera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yadira","last_name":"Soto-Feliciano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michiel","last_name":"Vermeulen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Giorgio","last_name":"Oliviero","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chun-Wei","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Richard","last_name":"Phillips","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adrian","last_name":"Bracken","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerard","last_name":"Brien","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"37tgjx44g4","title":"mTORC1 Activation Requires DRAM-1 by Facilitating  Lysosomal Amino Acid Efflux. (Beaumatin et al., Molecular Cell, 2019)","description":"This dataset contains complete, uncropped versions of Western blots and raw, unprocessed immunofluorescence images present in the manuscript \"mTORC1 Activation Requires DRAM-1 by Facilitating Lysosomal Amino Acid Efflux.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-03T15:41:49.202Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/37tgjx44g4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/37tgjx44g4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Florian","last_name":"Beaumatin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Valentin","last_name":"Barthet","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biological Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j4xzv3fcgj","title":"Data for: Oxidative stress, lysosomal damage and dysfunction of autophagy in molluscan hepatopancreas (digestive gland) induced by chemical contaminants   ","description":"Autophagy is a highly conserved evolutionary survival or defence process that enables cells and organisms to survive periods of environmental stress by breaking down cellular organelles and macromolecules in autolysosomes to provide a supply of nutrients for cell maintenance. However, autophagy is also a part of normal cellular physiology that facilitates the turnover of cellular constituents under normal conditions: it can be readily augmented by mild environmental stress; but becomes dysfunctional with severe oxidative stress leading to cellular pathology. The molluscan hepatopancreas or digestive gland provides a versatile and environmentally relevant model to investigate lysosomal autophagy and stress-induced dysfunctional autophagy.  This latter process has been implicated in many animal and human disease conditions, including degenerative and neurodegenerative diseases, as well as obesity related conditions. Many environmental pollutants have also been found to induce dysfunctional autophagy in molluscan hepatopancreatic digestive cells, and in this study, the marine blue mussel Mytilus galloprovincialis was exposed for 7 days to: 0.1 µM, 1 µM and 10 µM concentrations of fluoranthene and phenanthrene (PAHs); chlorpyrifos and malathion (organophosphorus compounds); atrazine (triazine herbicide); copper (transition metal) and dodecylbenzene sulphonic acid (LAS, surfactant). The marine snail or periwinkle, Littorina littorea, was also exposed to phenanthrene, chlorpyrifos and copper. Indices of oxidative stress, cell injury and dysfunctional autophagy were measured (i.e., lysosomal membrane stability, protein carbonyls, lipofuscin, and lysosomal accumulation of lipid or lipidosis). Evidence of oxidative stress, based on the elevation of lipofuscin and protein carbonyls, was found for all compounds tested; with chlorpyrifos being the most toxic to both species. Dysfunctional autophagy was induced by all of the compounds tested in both species, except for atrazine in mussels. This failure of normal autophagy was consistently associated with oxidative stress. A conceptual mechanistic model has been developed for dysregulation of autophagy, together with a new diagnostic/prognostic system (Euler logical relationship model) for assessing health status based on lysosomal biomarkers and predicting the pathophysiological outcome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-10-24T00:25:53.047Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j4xzv3fcgj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j4xzv3fcgj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael N","last_name":"Moore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa","last_name":"Al-Moosawi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhuofan","last_name":"Mou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bill","last_name":"Langston","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Frickers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Readman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Lowe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amanda","last_name":"Beesley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christine","last_name":"Pascoe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jennifer","last_name":"Shaw","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Environmental Toxicology","Environmental Pathology","Marine Ecotoxicology"],"journal_issn":["01411136"]},{"id":"jswn7xm4d3","title":"PGE1 protects against dopaminergic neurodegeneration by suppressing PRL-mediated autophagy via JAK/STAT and PI3K/AKT pathways","description":"Background and Purpose: Parkinson’s disease (PD) is the second most common neurodegenerative disorder, yet disease-modifying therapies remain limited. Prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) has shown neuroprotective effects in PD models, but its underlying mechanisms are not fully understood. This study aimed to investigate whether PGE1 exerts neuroprotection by modulating prolactin (PRL) release and the JAK/STAT and PI3K/AKT signaling pathways.\nMethods: MPP⁺-treated MN9D cells and MPTP-induced acute PD mouse models were used. Neuroprotective effects were assessed by cell viability, TUNEL apoptosis assay, behavioral tests, and tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) immunohistochemistry. Transcriptomic sequencing was performed to identify key targets. PRL was knocked down by siRNA, and pathway involvement was examined using WP1066 (JAK2 inhibitor) and Western blotting. Autophagy was evaluated by LC3-II/I ratio, p62 levels, and transmission electron microscopy.\nResults: PGE1 attenuated MPP⁺-induced apoptosis and dopaminergic neuronal loss, and alleviated MPTP-induced motor deficits. Transcriptomic analysis identified PRL as a significantly upregulated target in PD models, which was reversed by PGE1. PRL knockdown enhanced PGE1’s protective effects against TH reduction, α-syn accumulation, and apoptosis. Mechanistically, PGE1 suppressed MPP⁺/MPTP-induced hyperphosphorylation of JAK2, STAT3, PI3K, and AKT. PRL acted upstream of both pathways, and JAK2/STAT3 functioned upstream of PI3K/AKT. PGE1 also reduced aberrant autophagy, as evidenced by decreased LC3-II/I ratio and p62 levels, and this effect was amplified by PRL knockdown.\nConclusions: PGE1 protects against dopaminergic neurodegeneration in PD by inhibiting PRL release, thereby suppressing the JAK2/STAT3 and PI3K/AKT pathways and attenuating excessive autophagy. These findings identify PRL as a potential therapeutic target and support the repurposing of PGE1 for PD treatment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-05T10:15:00.633Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jswn7xm4d3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jswn7xm4d3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiabing","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Prostaglandin","Intracerebral Hemorrhage"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"92g4p2dftc","title":"Data related to article “Lfng-expressing centroacinar cell is a unique cell-of-origin for p53 deficient pancreatic cancer”","description":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is one of the most lethal malignancies with limited understanding of etiology. Studies in mice showed that both acinar and ductal cells of the pancreas can be targeted by combination of oncogenic Kras and p53 mutations to form PDAC. How the transforming capacities of pancreatic cells are constrained, and whether a subset of cells could serve as a prime target for oncogenic transformation, remain obscure. Here we report that expression of a Notch modulator, Lunatic Fringe (Lfng), is restricted to a limited number of cells with centroacinar location and morphology in the adult pancreas. Lfng-expressing cells are preferentially targeted by oncogenic Kras along with p53 deletion to form PDAC, and deletion of Lfng blocks tumor initiation from these cells. Notch3 is a functional Notch receptor for PDAC initiation and progression in this context. Lfng is upregulated in acinar- and ductal-derived PDAC and its deletion suppresses these tumors. Taken together, Lfng marks a centroacinar subpopulation that is uniquely susceptible to oncogenic transformation when p53 is lost, and Lfng functions as an oncogene in all three lineages of the exocrine pancreas.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-24T07:46:11.371Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/92g4p2dftc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/92g4p2dftc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Keli","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R01CA276347"}],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n9p8yjdxd9","title":"Membrane protective role of autophagic machinery during infection of epithelial cells by Candida albicans","description":"Membrane protective role of autophagic machinery during infection of epithelial cells by Candida albicans.\n\nCandida albicans (C. albicans) is an opportunistic pathogen causing infections ranging from superficial to life-threatening disseminated infections. In a susceptible host, C. albicans is able to translocate through the gut barrier, promoting its dissemination into deeper organs. C. albicans hyphae can invade human epithelial cells by two well-documented mechanisms: epithelial-driven endocytosis and C. albicans-driven active penetration. One mechanism by which host cells protect themselves against intracellular C. albicans is termed autophagy. The protective role of autophagy during C. albicans infection has been investigated in myeloid cells, however, far less is known regarding the role of this process during the infection of epithelial cells. In the present study we investigated the role of autophagy-related proteins during the infection of epithelial cells, including intestinal epithelial cells and gut explants, by C. albicans. Using cell imaging, we show that key molecular players of the autophagy machinery (LC3-II, PI3P, ATG16L1 and WIPI2) were recruited at Candida invasion sites. We deepened these observations by electron microscopy analyses that reveal presence of autophagosomes at the vicinity of invading hyphae. Importantly, these events occur during active penetration of C. albicans into host cells and are associated with plasma membrane damages. In this context, we show that the autophagy-related key-proteins ATG5 and ATG16L1 contribute to plasma membrane repair mediated by lysosomal exocytosis and participate in protecting epithelial cells against C. albicans-induced cell death. Our findings provide a novel mechanism by which epithelial cells, forming the first line of defense against C. albicans in the gut, can react to limit C. albicans invasion.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-01T14:33:46.302Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n9p8yjdxd9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n9p8yjdxd9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pierre","last_name":"Lapaquette","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Autophagy","Candida Infection"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139275,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":177,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139275,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138710,"restricted":64,"embargoed":501},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3556},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2320},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":658},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":475},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":411},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":376},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":279}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136636},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1903},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":135},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":76},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":3028},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":2344},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":504},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}